rables and signs and people well  what does that mean and what's going on  with this so these are things that  people are puzzling out and there wasn't  a rigid distinction as it turns out  later on between like okay so here on  this side is philosophy and the life of  reason here on this side is theology and  the life of blind faith that's a  nonsense idea that comes from later  crappy writers from the Enlightenment we  had axes to grind there there always  will be and there always have been some  people who do treat things that way even  in the high middle ages we have Peter  Damien saying even grammar is a tool of  the devil right but they're the  exception rather than the rule if you  look at these early Christian thinkers  they were thinkers they were attempting  to think out the world in new  circumstances within an ever-changing  culture that had already now hundreds of  years behind it and they're trying to  make make a new way of life a rational  coherent way of life out of it and so  they're doing philosophy philosophy back  then essentially meant a not just  intellectual exercise it meant a way of  committing yourself and living things  out and Christianity comes to be viewed  as one more philosophy among others as a  matter of fact it wants monasticism  real going concern so we're talking  about sort of the end of this era moving  into the early Middle Ages what the  monks were doing was often called  Christian philosophy philosophy as a way  of life so that's one thing that's going  on another thing that that you could say  is is the key part of their their  motivations is engaging in polemics  there is a lot of shared cultural space  people are jockeying for position in it  there there are current you know pagan  philosophers out there claiming that  this new religious point of view is  actually irrational and doesn't make  sense or is bad for the Empire which was  one of callouses claims and it's  actually one of the things motivating  Justin Martyr to write his apologies  saying that Christianity is actually  good for the Empire in some ways because  the Christians are being persecuted so  there's all of that going on all of this  engagements and then there's also what  we can call intra Christian struggles  and rivalries they're trying to clarify  what their beliefs actually are and so  they get into arguments with it some of  them will actually fall into what are  considered heresies we'll talk about  that word in just a minute  like for example Tara tolian he is a  extreme rigorous he argues against these  other positions eventually he becomes a  montanus now a heresy nowadays we look  at this word heresy and we're like oh  yeah that's something Christians use or  religious people use to oppress other  people hi racists originally means  choice and it means a choice of a  particular way of life so you know it  was used to describe philosophical  schools it's also used to describe  religious communities like that of the  Manicheans or that of the montanus or  that of the Aryans all of these are high  racists choices commitments ways of  living and a good part of early  Christian thought the Orthodox thought  that we're talking about here is aimed  at combating what they view as erroneous  understandings of not just Christianity  but the things that it bears upon like  the notion of whether we have free will  or not or whether the Incarnation  matters or doesn't matter whether you  know what kind of what does God like is  God finite infinite all of these sorts  of things are issues that they're  hashing out and they're not just doing  it in theological councils they're doing  it in philosophical works as well like  one of the key things that I really find  fascinating about this time period is  they are they are trying to hash out the  notion of free will and what it means so  these are all background assumptions  that you want to keep in mind as you're  reading these these thinkers you should  assume that they probably know more  about ancient philosophy then than you  likely do as a reader and that they had  access to texts that we no longer have  text from the Epicureans or the Stoics  or the skeptics or even lost works by  other Aristotelian zorp lateness the  last thing I want to say about this is  that there's a there's a prevalent  misinterpretation that we'll will say  you know early Christian thought is all  basically neoplatonist they just took  Plato and they got his thoughts through  these you know watered-down glosses on  him and then they just assimilated it  because they they like Plato better than  the other thinkers again when you go  back to the text and you read it you see  that that is that is just not the case  at all they often did say well Plato is  closest to what we're doing but they  also appropriate things from the Stoics  they took on some things occasionally  from the Aristotelian tradition  they're pretty conversant with the  history of philosophy this notion that  they were just like you know sort of you  know  grafting Christianity on the Platonic  philosophy pure nonsense they they did  in fact engage with a lot of the middle  and and and neoplatonism of the time  which itself was incredibly eclectic so  it's it's you know you have to have sort  of a more realistic view on what's going  on and in order to do that you've got to  go to the texts there's no way around  that and you've got to approach them  with fairly unblinking eyes so you don't  want to rely too much on somebody else's  history of ideas because that will give  you preconceptions that will make it  harder for you to appreciate what's  actually in front of you  I also think it's important to give a  bit of advice that stems from the let's  call it genres or contexts of these  works philosophy is an interesting field  in that there this despite the the  attempts of some people to try to say  philosophy has to be done in this format  all you got to do is like read a little  bit in the history of philosophy to  realize that that's complete nonsense  anybody who's trying to sell you the  idea that philosophy has to be done in  the in the form of the treatise or the  dialogue or the academic article is  either stupid or you know intellectually  lazy or trying to sell you something or  puffed up with pride or whatever there's  something wrong and going on there and  so you know when it comes to these sorts  of texts you see them doing some of the  same things that we see other ancient  philosophers doing writing dialogues for  example but also writing longer treaty  sees that are arranged in books a book  would be a scroll and then there'd be  chapters within the book but they can be  incredibly diverse in their approaches  to things so you'll find you'll find  actual dialogues where people are  talking with each other sometimes there  are more literary sometimes less so I  did mention that that you know account  of the argument between a philosopher I  want to say it's FN aghoris and and  monney as an interesting example of a  kind of dialogue it's supposed to be  like an actual transcript of a debate  apologies are another thing you know  we've got this term apologetics and  nowadays it's come to mean something  like starting from the standpoint of  faith and just trying to find arguments  for it to convince people but an apology  originally was a defense speech Socrates  apology right is Socrates defending  himself and Justin martyrs apologies are  his attempt to defend the Christians  against Roman officials who  really understand what the Christians  believe and stand for so he's trying to  explain to them why the Christians are  not an actual threat to the Roman Empire  and don't deserve persecution there are  a number of other interesting formats  the one thing that I think you want to  keep in mind is you're looking at this  stuff  not so much to try to appreciate the  prose itself or the format there is one  exception to this that I'll mention well  there's two exceptions to this that I'll  mention in just a minute you're you're  looking at it for the content you're  looking at it for what they're actually  saying so don't allow these genre or the  style to lead you off track if you think  that there's too many scripture quotes  sprinkled in there don't worry about  those the first read don't worry about  that just go on to the philosophical  content the real meat now I mentioned  that there are a few exceptions and I  was only going to talk about two that  are that are particularly noteworthy in  pre Augustinian writing because  Augustine himself is an amazing writer  one would be lactantius  locked on see is is is is a wonderful  writer in Latin well worth reading and  studying for his own sake but also for  the sake of the ideas and then there's  of course John Chrysostom if you know  what Chrysostom means it means golden  mouth the guy who could really talk well  and so you know you can imagine the  stuff that he says this is quite quite  good stylistically so you know reading  them can be quite nice I I really  enjoyed clement of alexandria you know  John Cassian is doing another sort of  thing he's got those conferences as I  mentioned with with the various Abbott's  and monks and then he's got the  Institute's where he's laying out sort  of a blueprint for what a monastery  ought to look like and how they had a  structure their days and then what are  the capital vices that cause us all  these troubles so  you know the long and short of it is for  you you just look at the format or the  genre of the work and try not to allow  your your preconceptions about what a  philosophical work ought to look like  keep you from getting into it and you  know when there's good style appreciate  that  I do want to once again give a few  cautions about what not to allow  yourself to get drawn into or to worried  about because it'll get in the way of  reading and appreciating and  understanding the works you can  certainly you know come back to them and  bring all these preoccupations if you  want to but I think on a first read  it'll be helpful for you to suspend or  bracket or put them aside so the first  one is you know is this stuff philosophy  at all because it doesn't fit a  definition of philosophy that we you  know that you're used to so if you for  example came up in a a sort of  philosophical tradition where you were  told that having any sort of faith  commitments nullified it as philosophy  you you you're really gonna have to put  that aside or you can't read this stuff  effectively because it you know  philosophy if we think about  philosophies history it's  extraordinarily broad there is no  consensus agreement on exactly what  counts as philosophy what doesn't count  as philosophy and the very notion of  drawing a dividing line between  philosophy and theology or philosophy  and faith or philosophy and religion  that is a later concept that comes about  in the Middle Ages and it's essentially  a theological position itself or if it's  a philosophical position it's a pretty  weird one it's become quite popular as  as you know philosophy became more and  more secularized in the modern period  but it's you know it really represents a  set of dogmatic assumptions that do need  to be called into question when we look  at the broad history of philosophy and  what's counted as philosophy because if  you think that having any sort of  religious commitments precludes you from  doing philosophy then Aristotle isn't  philosophy nor is Cicero nor  are any of the Stoics nor does Plato see  nor is Descartes nor you know you have  to leave so much aside you have to throw  out basically all the babies with the  bathwater and you're just left with a  crappy bathtub you know so so that's  that's one big one right there you also  probably don't need to know in and out  you know every one of the philosophical  schools or thinkers that these people  are engaging with so you you don't have  to track everything down as I've said so  many other times with with other  traditions and thinkers expect to read  and reread and reread and get more and  more out of it each time so you can  slowly build up the network of the  interconnection what we call  intertextual intertextuality where our  texts are referring to other texts and  philosophers other philosophers so that  that is another key issue the other  thing to keep in mind too is I think a  lot of people they worry when they're  reading religious thinkers well what if  I don't I don't believe you know or what  if I'm actually like a committed atheist  or what if I'm what if I belong to a  different religion is this going to pose  a problem for me and some people I think  are actually afraid that they might get  converted that could happen through  reading these people right but more  often it's a matter of like coming to  them already can on guard and suspicious  and not wanting to see where the  thoughts go and being a little bit too  prone to say aha  that's just their religious commitment  there well I'm not saying you can't do  that that you know it's you're free to  do whatever you want but if you approach  things with that sort of mentality you  won't get out of it what what other  people do you don't have to believe in  any of this stuff in order to  recognize the interesting approaches and  value of these texts and thinkers I mean  any more than you have to like buy into  dualism to appreciate the cart or you  know accept Kant's you know distinction  between the phenomenal and the the  nominal in order to appreciate Kant and  I and I think you know it's interesting  there's there's people who are more  willing to give a non-religious  philosopher the benefit of the doubt to  see where they're going then they are  with religious philosophers like I said  you you could get converted by this but  it's probably you know rather rare that  that happens and if it were to happen it  